    {
      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment of an OIML certificate.\n\nDocument-level info:\n- Number: R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01, revision 10\n- Scheme: A\n- Project number: 3744031\n- Page total: 13\n- Member State: The Netherlands\n- Date issued: 26 February 2024\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1\n\nIssuing authority:\n- Name: NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\n- Address: Thijsseweg 11, 2629 JA Delft, The Netherlands\n- Person responsible: M.Ph.D. Schmidt\n- Phone: +31 88 636 2332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1\n\nApplicants:\n- ABB Limited, Oldends Lane, GL10 3TA Stonehouse, United Kingdom\n\nManufacturers: same as applicant (Applicant and Manufacturer: ABB Limited)\n- ABB Limited, same address\n\nCertified type:\n- Category: Water meter (electromagnetic water meter). Category options given: \"Load cell\", \"Indicator\", \"Measuring system\", \"Fuel dispenser\", \"Taximeter\", \"Gas meter\", ... — water meter fits. I'll use \"Water meter\".\n- Type designations: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4* — the type includes patterns. Types: \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4\" (FET4XY.Z presumably). The text says \"Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 *\". So type designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4XY.Z\"]? Actually FET4 * — hmm. Let me keep [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4\"]. The asterisk refers to the note about X, Y, Z variants. I'll include both \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\" and \"FET4\" (maybe \"FET4XY.Z\"?). The markdown says \"Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 \\*\". So the literal is \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 *\". I'll put [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4\"].\n- Module designation: For water meters, modules... not applicable. The certificate doesn't mention module. Omit or null. I'll omit (rule 8: don't fabricate). Actually could set null. The schema allows module_designation: \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null. I'll omit since not present.\n- Description: \"An electromagnetic water meter\" — the identification says that.\n\nModel family: Yes — the cert covers multiple model variants:\n- X: 1 (standard), 3 (advanced), 5 (premium mobile comms MC)\n- Y: 1, 2, 8, 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only)\n- Z: R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore), F (full bore)\n\nTables 1 and 2 give characteristics for FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z and FEW45Y.Z variants.\n\nModels: We could model per the FEW41Y.Z / FEW43Y.Z / FEW45Y.Z variants since tables differ. Also FET variants. But the family name is \"AquaMaster4\". Models could be:\n- \"FEW41Y.Z\" (standard)\n- \"FEW43Y.Z\" (advanced)\n- \"FEW45Y.Z\" (premium MC)\n- \"FET4XY.Z\"?\n\nHmm, FET4 — the \"*\" applies to both. Actually the type is \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 *\". FET4 is likely the transmitter type designation (FET4 = flow transmitter?). Actually in ABB AquaMaster, FEW is the flow sensor (electromagnetic water) and FET is... hmm. AquaMaster4: FEW = electromagnetic flow sensor; FET = ? Possibly \"flow electronic transmitter\"? Given \"for both flow sensor and flow transmitter\", FET4 is the transmitter. The certificate identifies the type as AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4*.\n\nI'll include type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4\"].\n\nModel family: family_name \"AquaMaster4\", models with attributes:\n- model_id \"FEW41Y.Z\", label \"Standard variant\"\n- model_id \"FEW43Y.Z\", label \"Advanced variant\"\n- model_id \"FEW45Y.Z\", label \"Premium variant (mobile Comms - MC)\"\n\nCharacteristics type_level (common): measuring principle electromagnetic, accuracy class \"1 and 2\", environmental class O, mechanical class M1, temperature range ambient -25/+55 °C, water temperature class T50 (+0.1/+50 °C), MAP 1.6 MPa (16 bar), orientation all positions, reverse flow yes, checking facilities Type P.\n\nSome vary between models:\n- Electromagnetic environment: E2 for FEW41/43; E1 for transmitter with external antenna cable, E2 without — for FEW45. This varies per model → model_level entry.\n- Power supply: differs — FEW41/43: AC mains 95–240 VAC @ 50/60 Hz; renewable energy DC 6–32 V; replaceable battery 2x 3.6 V (operating range 2.9–3.6 V). FEW45: AC mains including 4x 3.6 V replaceable batteries; DC 6–32 V including batteries; battery 6x 3.6 V.\n- Flow profile sensitivity class: same for both tables: U0 and D0, except DN250 and larger: U3 and D0. This is config-level (varies by DN size) — could be config_level with axis \"nominal_size\".\n- Pressure loss class: varies by size and bore type — config_level with axis combining bore type and DN. This is complex; maybe represent as matrix or config_level.\n\nLet me structure:\n\ncharacteristics.type_level:\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic\"\n- accuracy_class: \"1 and 2\"\n- environmental_class: \"O (installed outdoors)\"\n- mechanical_class: \"M1\"\n- temperature_range_ambient: {min:-25, max:55}, unit °C\n- water_temperature_class: \"T50\" with value? T50 (+0.1 °C / +50 °C). Could represent: water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"} and water_temperature_range: {min 0.1, max 50, unit °C}. The +0.1 is lower bound. Range {min: 0.1, max: 50}.\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa, and maybe 16 bar note. Value 1.6 unit \"MPa\". Footnote none.\n- orientation: \"All positions\"\n- reverse_flow: \"intended to measure reverse flow\" — value string \"yes\"? Keep as text: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\" → value \"intended to measure reverse flow\"? I'd write value: true? Hmm. Use string.\n- checking_facilities: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\"\n\nconfig_level:\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: axis \"nominal_size\" (DN), values: condition \"DN < 250\" → \"U0/D0\", condition \"DN ≥ 250\" → \"U3/D0\". The conditions: \"up to DN200\" vs \"DN250 and larger\". Represent:\n  - {condition: \"full bore size DN < 250\", value: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\"}\n  - {condition: \"full bore size DN ≥ 250\", value: \"U3 and D0 (3 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\"}\n- pressure_loss_class: axis \"bore type / size\". Values with conditions:\n  - reduced bore, DN40–DN50: Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\n  - reduced bore, DN > 50: Δp 63 (0.63 bar)\n  - (virtual) full bore, DN40–DN50: Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\n  - (virtual) full bore, DN65–DN200: Δp 16 (0.16 bar)\n  - (virtual) full bore, DN ≥ 250: Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\n\nValues: pressure_loss_class value \"Δp 40\" with pressure_loss max 0.40 bar. The value could be string \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\". I'll use value \"Δp 40\" and maybe include numeric? The structured rule says values must be objects with value/unit. For config_level values shape: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. Numeric value for Δp class: could give max_pressure_loss 0.40 bar. Hmm, I'll represent value as the numeric pressure loss in bar and add a separate field? The schema for config_level values: {condition, value}. I'll put value as string \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\" to preserve, or better: value 0.40 with unit \"bar\" and attribute pressure_loss... but the class designation Δp40 matters. I'll do two config_level entries? That may be over-engineering. I'll do one with value strings.\n\nmodel_level:\n- electromagnetic_environment: values: {model \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"E2\"}, {model \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"E2\"}, {model \"FEW45Y.Z\", \"E1 (transmitter with external antenna cable) / E2 (transmitter without external antenna cable)\"}\n- battery_count: 2x 3.6V vs 6x 3.6V → battery_configuration: values {FEW41Y.Z/FEW43Y.Z: \"2 × 3.6 V (operating range 2.9–3.6 V)\", FEW45Y.Z: \"6 × 3.6 V (operating range 2.9–3.6 V)\"}. As structured value: number? \"2x\" is count. Could do battery_count 2 vs 6, battery_voltage 3.6 V. That's cleaner:\n  - replaceable_battery_count: {model FEW41Y.Z: 2, FEW43Y.Z: 2, FEW45Y.Z: 6}, unit null (count) or unit \"cells\"? unit_symbol null.\n  - battery_voltage: 3.6 V for all → could be type_level (3.6 V both) — both tables say 3,6 V. So type_level: replaceable_battery_voltage 3.6 V, operating range 2.9–3.6 V.\n- ac_mains_includes_batteries for FEW45 — that's a remark; skip or capture in power supply differences. FEW41/43 power supply: AC mains 95–240 VAC @ 50/60 Hz. FEW45: same \"including 4x 3.6 V replaceable batteries\". Core values same, so type_level: power_supply_voltage_ac {min 95, max 240} V; power_supply_frequency {min 50, max 60} Hz; dc_supply_voltage {min 6, max 32} V.\n\nmodel_level for power supply nuance: maybe skip the \"including batteries\" nuance; could add attribute \"power_supply_includes_replaceable_batteries\" for FEW45Y.Z true. Hmm — might be too granular. I'll include model_level entry for battery count and note.\n\nmatrix_tables: Table 3 software identification — that's a table of software versions. Should I include as matrix_table? It's a multi-row table: software part codes with versions and CRC checksums. Yes, preserve as matrix_tables entry: name \"Software identification for AquaMaster4 FEW41Y.Z and AquaMaster4 FEW43Y.Z variant\", columns [software_part_code, software_version, crc_checksum, remarks], rows grouped by part code.\n\nRows:\n- part code 3KXF208402U0113 (Full functionality): versions 03.00.03/B26AFE6B, 03.00.04/6E98963A, 03.02.00/E12E5C04, 03.03.02/D4D1E40B (HMI display bug fixes, low flow output bug fix), 03.03.03/CEEF45F1 (Modbus communication bug fix), 03.03.06/DAC51328, 03.04.00/277BA336.\n- 3KXF208402U0313 (Sensus / Logger / Pressure / VKL sensor updater): 02.00.02/84EBD1C8.\n- 3KXF208402U0513 (Modbus / Pressure / VKL sensor updater): 02.00.01/C28EAA16.\n\nTable 4 header appears but content is cut off (fragment ends). Footnote [1] marker on Table 4 but no definition present. I shouldn't fabricate the footnote text. Table 4 content not present — omit. Maybe include footnote marker? No, definition missing. Skip.\n\nrecommendation: R49-1, edition 2013? \"R 49-1 (2013)\". Canonical id \"R49\" (per rule: canonical, e.g. \"R60\"). The recommendation is R 49-1 — part 1 of R49. I'd use id \"R49-1\" or \"R49\"? The rule says canonical e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". For R49-1, part-specific: \"R49-1\". Edition: 2013. Hmm, \"edition (int)\": 2013. Amendment: none. Scheme: A. Accuracy classes: [\"1\", \"2\"].\n\ntest_reports:\n- NMi-2254799-01, 2020-08-14, 235 pages, role? These are test/evaluation reports. Role: \"type_evaluation_report\"? The certificate says \"tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\". Also \"OIML Type Evaluation Report\" mentioned. These report numbers are likely the Type Evaluation Report (with 235 pages). I'll mark them \"type_evaluation_report\" for the first (which represents the sample in OIML Type Evaluation Report?) — Hard to know which. The doc says \"represented by the sample(s) identified in the OIML Type Evaluation Report\". Multiple reports listed as evidence. I'll set role \"test_report\" for all, or \"type_evaluation_report\"? Given they were used to establish conformity, likely type evaluation reports. I'll use \"type_evaluation_report\" for the ones... hmm, can't distinguish. I'll use \"test_report\" for all to be safe? The role enum: type_evaluation_report | test_report | documentation_file | evaluation_report. The first list item likely the main Type Evaluation Report (235 pages). Others could be additional test reports. Since indistinguishable, I'll mark all as \"type_evaluation_report\"? Risky. Let me think: The text: \"The conformity was established by the results of tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\". These are test reports supporting the certificate. I'll use \"test_report\" for all, except maybe the first which is likely the Type Evaluation Report itself. Actually, in NMi certs, \"No. NMi-2254799-01 dated 14 August 2020 that includes 235 pages\" — the type evaluation report. Subsequent ones with different numbers are additional test reports (e.g., software assessment, EMC). I'll set the first as type_evaluation_report and the rest as test_report. Hmm, but this is guesswork. Alternatively mark all \"type_evaluation_report\". I'll go with first = type_evaluation_report, others = test_report.\n\nAlso documentation folder: \"The construction of the measuring instrument is recorded in the Documentation folder no. T11851-8.\" → test_reports entry: id \"T11851-8\", role \"documentation_file\". No date/pages.\n\nrevision_history: revision \"10\" (string), date 2024-02-26? The date on cert is 26 February 2024 — that's issue date of revision 10. Changes not stated. Include revision \"10\", date \"2024-02-26\", changes omitted? Schema: revision, date, changes. changes unknown — omit changes? Rule: don't fabricate. I'll include {\"revision\": \"10\", \"date\": \"2024-02-26\"} without changes. Actually maybe omit revision_history since it's not explicitly stated as revision history — but the number includes \"revision 10\" and date given. I'll include it with changes omitted... The schema requires changes field? \"revision (string, e.g. \"0\" not 0), date, changes\". If unknown, null. I'll include with \"changes\": null.\n\ncertificate.date_issued: \"2024-02-26\".\ncertificate.page_total: 13.\ncertificate.oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\".\nscheme: \"A\".\n\nUnits: MAP 1,6 MPa → 1.6 MPa (also 16 bar noted). I'll value 1.6 unit MPa, maybe second characteristic? Keep one with note? The schema structured value doesn't have notes. I could add \"maximum_admissible_pressure_bar\": 16. Eh, I'll include just MPa.\n\nTemperature water: T50 class, range +0.1 to +50 °C → water_temperature_class type_level string \"T50\"; water_temperature_range {min:0.1, max:50} °C.\n\nFlow profile: config_level axis \"nominal_size (full bore)\".\n\nPressure loss: config_level axis \"bore_type_and_nominal_size\". Conditions:\n- \"Reduced bore, DN40–DN50\": \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\"\n- \"Reduced bore, larger than DN50\": \"Δp 63 (0.63 bar)\"\n- \"(Virtual) full bore, DN40–DN50\": \"Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\"\n- \"(Virtual) full bore, DN65–DN200\": \"Δp 16 (0.16 bar)\"\n- \"(Virtual) full bore, DN250 and larger\": \"Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\"\n\nValues as strings here (class designations). Structured form demands {condition, value, unit_symbol} — value can be string. OK.\n\nModel family models: Should I include FET4? The models list — the cert covers FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z (X=1,3,5) with Y ∈ {1,2,8,9}, Z ∈ {R,V,F}. Full expansion would be 3×4×3 = 36 FEW variants plus FET. Too many; I'll model three model_ids based on X variants, and note Y/Z axes in attributes. models:\n- {\"model_id\": \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"label\": \"Standard (X=1)\", \"attributes\": {\"y_versions\": \"1, 2, 8, 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only)\", \"z_versions\": \"R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore), F (full bore)\"}}\n- {\"model_id\": \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"label\": \"Advanced (X=3)\", ...}\n- {\"model_id\": \"FEW45Y.Z\", \"label\": \"Premium, mobile Comms MC (X=5)\", ...}\n- Maybe {\"model_id\": \"FET4\", \"label\": \"Flow transmitter\"}? FET4 is part of the type. Include as component? The cert mentions flow sensor and flow transmitter. Components: flow sensor FEW4XY.Z and transmitter FET4. Hmm — \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4\" — FET4 might be remote transmitter version designation? Actually ABB AquaMaster3: FEW = sensor, FET = transmitter (remote). I'll add components: role \"flow sensor\" type [\"FEW4XY.Z\"], role \"transmitter\" type [\"FET4\"], alternatives? Not alternatives — both named. Since uncertain, maybe keep simple: components with role \"flow sensor (FEW)\" and \"flow transmitter (FET)\". The certificate says \"(for both flow sensor and flow transmitter)\". I think it's safe to include components:\n- {role: \"flow sensor\", type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\"]}\n- {role: \"transmitter\", type_designations: [\"FET4\"]}\n\nHmm, rule 6 says components for complex instruments when cert names sub-assemblies. It does name FEW4XY.Z and FET4. I'll include.\n\nNow build JSON.\n\ncertificate:\n```json\n{\n  \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\",\n  \"revision\": \"10\",\n  \"scheme\": \"A\",\n  \"project_number\": \"3744031\",\n  \"page_total\": 13,\n  \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\",\n  \"date_issued\": \"2024-02-26\",\n  \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n}\n```\n\nissuing_authority:\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\",\n  \"address_lines\": [\"Thijsseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"The Netherlands\"],\n  \"person_responsible\": \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\",\n  \"phone\": \"+31 88 636 2332\",\n  \"email\": \"certin@nmi.nl\",\n  \"website\": \"www.nmi.nl\",\n  \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n}\n```\nperson_title: not given (Certification Board? Not a title). Omit.\n\napplicants: [{name: \"ABB Limited\", address_lines: [\"Oldends Lane\", \"GL10 3TA Stonehouse\", \"United Kingdom\"]}]\nmanufacturers: same.\n\ncertified_type:\n```json\n{\n  \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n  \"type_designations\": [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4\"],\n  \"description\": \"An electromagnetic water meter\"\n}\n```\n\nmodel_family:\n```json\n{\n  \"family_name\": \"AquaMaster4\",\n  \"models\": [\n    {\"model_id\": \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"label\": \"Standard variant (X=1)\", \"attributes\": {\"x_variant\": \"1 (standard)\", \"y_versions\": \"1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only)\", \"z_versions\": \"R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore)\"}},\n    {\"model_id\": \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"label\": \"Advanced variant (X=3)\", \"attributes\": {...}},\n    {\"model_id\": \"FEW45Y.Z\", \"label\": \"Premium variant with mobile Comms (X=5)\", \"attributes\": {...}}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\ncomponents:\n- {\"role\": \"flow sensor\", \"type_designations\": [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\"]}\n- {\"role\": \"flow transmitter\", \"type_designations\": [\"FET4\"]}\n\ncharacteristics.type_level:\n```json\n{\n  \"measuring_principle\": {\"value\": \"Electromagnetic\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"accuracy_class\": {\"value\": \"1 and 2\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"environmental_class\": {\"value\": \"O (installed outdoors)\", ...},\n  \"mechanical_class\": {\"value\": \"M1\", ...},\n  \"temperature_range_ambient\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -25, \"max\": 55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", ...},\n  \"water_temperature_class\": {\"value\": \"T50\", ...},\n  \"water_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 0.1, \"max\": 50}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n  \"maximum_admissible_pressure\": {\"value\": 1.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"MPa\"},\n  \"orientation\": {\"value\": \"All positions (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\"},\n  \"reverse_flow\": {\"value\": \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\"},\n  \"checking_facilities\": {\"value\": \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\"},\n  \"replaceable_battery_voltage\": {\"value\": 3.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n  \"replaceable_battery_operating_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 2.9, \"max\": 3.6}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n  \"power_supply_voltage_ac\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 95, \"max\": 240}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n  \"power_supply_frequency\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 50, \"max\": 60}, \"unit_symbol\": \"Hz\"},\n  \"dc_supply_voltage\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 6, \"max\": 32}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"}\n}\n```\n\nWait — battery voltage: FEW41/43 use 2× 3.6 V; FEW45 uses 6× 3.6 V; FEW45 AC/DC includes 4× 3.6 V. Battery voltage 3.6 common. Operating range 2.9–3.6 common. Fine at type level.\n\nAlso note: FEW45 AC mains \"including 4x 3.6 V replaceable batteries\" — internal batteries within unit. Might add model_level \"internal_replaceable_battery_count\": FEW45: 4? Hmm the 6x battery is the \"Replaceable Battery\" line for FEW45 (2x for FEW41/43). The 4x appears in the AC and DC lines. This is getting complicated; keep model_level for battery count: FEW41Y.Z: 2, FEW43Y.Z: 2, FEW45Y.Z: 6. And maybe note in attributes.\n\nmodel_level:\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"electromagnetic_environment\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"model\": \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"value\": \"E2\"},\n      {\"model\": \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"value\": \"E2\"},\n      {\"model\": \"FEW45Y.Z\", \"value\": \"E1 (transmitter with external antenna cable) / E2 (transmitter without external antenna cable)\"}\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"replaceable_battery_count\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"model\": \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"value\": 2},\n      {\"model\": \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"value\": 2},\n      {\"model\": \"FEW45Y.Z\", \"value\": 6}\n    ]\n  }\n]\n```\n\nconfig_level:\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\",\n    \"axis\": \"nominal size (full bore)\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"full bore size < DN250\", \"value\": \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\", \"unit_symbol\": null},\n      {\"condition\": \"full bore size DN250 and larger\", \"value\": \"U3 and D0 (3 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\", \"unit_symbol\": null}\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"pressure_loss_class\",\n    \"axis\": \"bore type and nominal size\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"Reduced bore, DN40 and DN50\", \"value\": \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\", \"unit_symbol\": null},\n      {\"condition\": \"Reduced bore, larger than DN50\", \"value\": \"Δp 63 (0.63 bar)\", \"unit_symbol\": null},\n      {\"condition\": \"(Virtual) full bore, DN40 and DN50\", \"value\": \"Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\", \"unit_symbol\": null},\n      {\"condition\": \"(Virtual) full bore, DN65 to DN200\", \"value\": \"Δp 16 (0.16 bar)\", \"unit_symbol\": null},\n      {\"condition\": \"(Virtual) full bore, DN250 and larger\", \"value\": \"Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\", \"unit_symbol\": null}\n    ]\n  }\n]\n```\n\nThe Δp values — should I convert to numeric with unit bar? e.g. value 0.40, unit bar, plus class \"Δp 40\"? Config values shape allows value as string. I'll keep strings with class designation. Alternatively split attribute: pressure_loss (max) numeric. I think keeping class designation string is fine and preserves info.\n\nmatrix_tables: software identification table:\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"Software identification for AquaMaster4 FEW41Y.Z and AquaMaster4 FEW43Y.Z variant\",\n  \"columns\": [\"software_part_code\", \"software_version\", \"crc_checksum\", \"remarks\"],\n  \"rows\": [\n    {\"software_part_code\": \"3KXF208402U0113 (Full functionality)\", \"software_version\": \"03.00.03\", \"crc_checksum\": \"B26AFE6B\", \"remarks\": null},\n    ...\n  ]\n}\n```\nI'll flatten each row with part code repeated (including function description).\n\nRows:\n1. 3KXF208402U0113 (Full functionality), 03.00.03, B26AFE6B, -\n2. same, 03.00.04, 6E98963A, -\n3. same, 03.02.00, E12E5C04, -\n4. same, 03.03.02, D4D1E40B, \"HMI display bug fixes. Low flow output bug fix.\"\n5. same, 03.03.03, CEEF45F1, \"Modbus communication bug fix.\"\n6. same, 03.03.06, DAC51328, -\n7. same, 03.04.00, 277BA336, -\n8. 3KXF208402U0313 (Sensus / Logger / Pressure / VKL sensor updater), 02.00.02, 84EBD1C8, -\n9. 3KXF208402U0513 (Modbus / Pressure / VKL sensor updater), 02.00.01, C28EAA16, -\n\nUse \"-\" as null for remarks.\n\nrecommendation:\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"R49-1\",\n  \"edition\": 2013,\n  \"scheme\": \"A\",\n  \"accuracy_classes\": [\"1\", \"2\"]\n}\n```\nHmm accuracy class 1 and 2 — for water meters R49 classes. OK.\n\ntest_reports:\n```json\n[\n  {\"id\": \"NMi-2254799-01\", \"date\": \"2020-08-14\", \"pages\": 235, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n  {\"id\": \"NMi-2254799-03\", \"date\": \"2020-09-30\", \"pages\": 44, \"role\": \"test_report\"},\n  {\"id\": \"NMi-2254799-04\", \"date\": \"2020-12-24\", \"pages\": 180, \"role\": \"test_report\"},\n  {\"id\": \"NMi-2564429-01\", \"date\": \"2021-08-02\", \"pages\": 26, \"role\": \"test_report\"},\n  {\"id\": \"NMi-2496280-01\", \"date\": \"2021-08-26\", \"pages\": 29, \"role\": \"test_report\"},\n  {\"id\": \"NMi-2496280-02\", \"date\": \"2021-11-23\", \"pages\": 25, \"role\": \"test_report\"},\n  {\"id\": \"NMi-2563125-01\", \"date\": \"2023-09-22\", \"pages\": 94, \"role\": \"test_report\"},\n  {\"id\": \"T11851-8\", \"role\": \"documentation_file\"}\n]\n```\n\nrevision_history: [{\"revision\": \"10\", \"date\": \"2024-02-26\", \"changes\": null}]\n\nfootnotes: The Table 4 title has sup [1] marker but no definition in fragment. Omit footnotes array (no definitions). Rule 7 says extract footnote definitions; none present.\n\nCertificate revision field: schema for certificate doesn't include revision — but revision_history covers it. The number could include \"revision 10\". I'll keep certificate.number as \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and add revision_history.\n\nDouble-check decimal conversions: 0,40 → 0.40 (0.4); 0,63 → 0.63; 0,10 → 0.10; 1,6 → 1.6; 3,6 → 3.6; +0,1 → 0.1.\n\nUnit for battery count — null.\n\nOutput single JSON object. Ensure no markdown fences.",
